<span data-as=3D"p"><a class=3D"link" href=3D"https://docs.langchain.com/os=
s/python/langgraph/add-memory#add-long-term-memory">Long-term memory</a> in=
 LangGraph allows systems to retain information across different conversati=
ons or sessions. Unlike short-term memory, which is <strong>thread-scoped</=
strong>, long-term memory is saved within custom =E2=80=9Cnamespaces.=E2=80=
=9D</span>
<span data-as=3D"p">Long-term memory is a complex challenge without a one-s=
ize-fits-all solution. However, the following questions provide a framework=
 to help you navigate the different techniques:</span>
<ul>
<li>What is the type of memory? Humans use memories to remember facts (<a h=
ref=3D"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/concepts/memory#semantic-memor=
y" class=3D"link">semantic memory</a>), experiences (<a href=3D"https://doc=
s.langchain.com/oss/python/concepts/memory#episodic-memory" class=3D"link">=
episodic memory</a>), and rules (<a href=3D"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/=
python/concepts/memory#procedural-memory" class=3D"link">procedural memory<=
/a>). AI agents can use memory in the same ways. For example, AI agents can=
 use memory to remember specific facts about a user to accomplish a task.</=
li>
<li><a href=3D"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/concepts/memory#writin=
g-memories" class=3D"link">When do you want to update memories?</a> Memory =
can be updated as part of an agent=E2=80=99s application logic (e.g., =E2=
=80=9Con the hot path=E2=80=9D). In this case, the agent typically decides =
to remember facts before responding to a user. Alternatively, memory can be=
 updated as a background task (logic that runs in the background / asynchro=
nously and generates memories). We explain the tradeoffs between these appr=
oaches in the <a href=3D"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/concepts/mem=
ory#writing-memories" class=3D"link">section below</a>.</li>
</ul>
<span data-as=3D"p">Different applications require various types of memory.=
 Although the analogy isn=E2=80=99t perfect, examining <a href=3D"https://w=
ww.psychologytoday.com/us/basics/memory/types-of-memory?ref=3Dblog.langchai=
n.dev" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer" class=3D"link">human memory typ=
es</a> can be insightful. Some research (e.g., the <a href=3D"https://arxiv=
.org/pdf/2309.02427" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer" class=3D"link">Co=
ALA paper</a>) have even mapped these human memory types to those used in A=
I agents.</span>
