<span class=3D"cursor-pointer">Collection</span></h4>
<span data-as=3D"p">Alternatively, memories can be a collection of document=
s that are continuously updated and extended over time. Each individual mem=
ory can be more narrowly scoped and easier to generate, which means that yo=
u=E2=80=99re less likely to <strong>lose</strong> information over time. It=
=E2=80=99s easier for an LLM to generate <em>new</em> objects for new infor=
mation than reconcile new information with an existing profile. As a result=
, a document collection tends to lead to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Precision_and_recall" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer" class=3D"=
link">higher recall downstream</a>.</span>
<span data-as=3D"p">However, this shifts some complexity memory updating. T=
he model must now <em>delete</em> or <em>update</em> existing items in the =
list, which can be tricky. In addition, some models may default to over-ins=
erting and others may default to over-updating. See the <a href=3D"https://=
github.com/hinthornw/trustcall" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer" class=
=3D"link">Trustcall</a> package for one way to manage this and consider eva=
luation (e.g., with a tool like <a href=3D"https://docs.smith.langchain.com=
/tutorials/Developers/evaluation" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer" clas=
s=3D"link">LangSmith</a>) to help you tune the behavior.</span>
<span data-as=3D"p">Working with document collections also shifts complexit=
y to memory <strong>search</strong> over the list. The <code>Store</code> c=
urrently supports both <a href=3D"https://langchain-ai.github.io/langgraph/=
reference/store/#langgraph.store.base.SearchOp.query" target=3D"_blank" rel=
=3D"noreferrer" class=3D"link">semantic search</a> and <a href=3D"https://l=
angchain-ai.github.io/langgraph/reference/store/#langgraph.store.base.Searc=
hOp.filter" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer" class=3D"link">filtering b=
y content</a>.</span>
<span data-as=3D"p">Finally, using a collection of memories can make it cha=
llenging to provide comprehensive context to the model. While individual me=
mories may follow a specific schema, this structure might not capture the f=
ull context or relationships between memories. As a result, when using thes=
e memories to generate responses, the model may lack important contextual i=
nformation that would be more readily available in a unified profile approa=
ch.</span>
