</a></div><span class=3D"cursor-pointer">Episodic memory</span></h3>
<span data-as=3D"p"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Episodic_memor=
y" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer" class=3D"link">Episodic memory</a>,=
 in both humans and AI agents, involves recalling past events or actions. T=
he <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.02427" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"no=
referrer" class=3D"link">CoALA paper</a> frames this well: facts can be wri=
tten to semantic memory, whereas <em>experiences</em> can be written to epi=
sodic memory. For AI agents, episodic memory is often used to help an agent=
 remember how to accomplish a task.</span>
<span data-as=3D"p">In practice, episodic memories are often implemented th=
rough few-shot example prompting, where agents learn from past sequences to=
 perform tasks correctly. Sometimes it=E2=80=99s easier to =E2=80=9Cshow=E2=
=80=9D than =E2=80=9Ctell=E2=80=9D and LLMs learn well from examples. Few-s=
hot learning lets you <a href=3D"https://x.com/karpathy/status/162736641384=
0322562" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer" class=3D"link">=E2=80=9Cprogr=
am=E2=80=9D</a> your LLM by updating the prompt with input-output examples =
to illustrate the intended behavior. While various best-practices can be us=
ed to generate few-shot examples, often the challenge lies in selecting the=
 most relevant examples based on user input.</span>
<span data-as=3D"p">Note that the memory <a class=3D"link" href=3D"https://=
docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/persistence#memory-store">store</a>=
 is just one way to store data as few-shot examples. If you want to have mo=
re developer involvement, or tie few-shots more closely to your evaluation =
harness, you can also use a <a class=3D"link" href=3D"https://docs.langchai=
n.com/langsmith/index-datasets-for-dynamic-few-shot-example-selection">Lang=
Smith Dataset</a> to store your data. Then dynamic few-shot example selecto=
rs can be used out-of-the box to achieve this same goal. LangSmith will ind=
ex the dataset for you and enable retrieval of few shot examples that are m=
ost relevant to the user input based upon keyword similarity (<a class=3D"l=
ink" href=3D"https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/index-datasets-for-dynami=
c-few-shot-example-selection">using a BM25-like algorithm</a> for keyword b=
ased similarity).</span>
<span data-as=3D"p">See this how-to <a href=3D"https://www.youtube.com/watc=
h?v=3D37VaU7e7t5o" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer" class=3D"link">vide=
o</a> for example usage of dynamic few-shot example selection in LangSmith.=
 Also, see this <a href=3D"https://blog.langchain.dev/few-shot-prompting-to=
-improve-tool-calling-performance/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer" cl=
ass=3D"link">blog post</a> showcasing few-shot prompting to improve tool ca=
lling performance and this <a href=3D"https://blog.langchain.dev/aligning-l=
lm-as-a-judge-with-human-preferences/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer"=
 class=3D"link">blog post</a> using few-shot example to align an LLMs to hu=
man preferences.</span>
